# License

IAC is licensed per artefact type, so that both spec implementers and studios
shipping commercial games can use it without legal friction.

| Path | Content | License | SPDX |
|------|---------|---------|------|
| `spec/`, `docs/`, `registry/`, `rfcs/`, `README*.md`, `GOVERNANCE.md` | Specification text and documentation | Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International | `CC-BY-4.0` |
| `tools/`, `testdata/` runner code, CI configuration | Reference implementation and test code | Apache License 2.0 | `Apache-2.0` |
| `examples/`, `testdata/` `.iac` files | Example and conformance data files | CC0 1.0 Universal (public domain dedication) | `CC0-1.0` |

Copyright (c) 2026 MIAR STAFF and IAC contributors.

## Why this split

- **CC BY 4.0 on the specification** lets anyone quote, reprint, translate and
  build on the normative text, including commercially, as long as attribution
  is preserved. It does not cover software, which is why the reference
  implementation is separate.
- **Apache-2.0 on the reference implementation** includes an explicit patent
  grant. Studios will not adopt a format whose reference parser has an unclear
  patent position.
- **CC0 on the data files** means a studio can copy an example cue sheet into a
  proprietary project with no attribution obligation and no licence audit.

## Attribution

When redistributing the specification or a derivative, credit:

> "IAC subtitle format specification, version 1.0, CC BY 4.0,
> https://github.com/<org>/iac-format"

## Before the public announcement

The full legal texts of CC BY 4.0, Apache-2.0 and CC0-1.0 must be vendored into
this repository from their canonical sources before the first public release;
the SPDX identifiers above are authoritative in the meantime. Tracked in
`ROADMAP.md` under v1.0.1.

## Contributions

By submitting a pull request you agree to license your contribution under the
licence of the directory it lands in. See `CONTRIBUTING.md`.
